Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
>Philipp Lucas wrote:
>> Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
>>>The 1000+ experience points when killing
>>>sea creatures that can drown you is for the added risk you take in this
>>>case. A sea creature on dry land is no risk at all and, furthermore, if
>>>you just wait their HP's will even drop to 1 HP. 
>> Sea creatures are not generated on land. If one uses up a charge of a
>> wand of teleportation to teleport a kraken onto dry land and kill it
>> there, it is as good a way of killing it as zapping it with magic
>> missiles from afar, which also protects against drowning. Why should the
>> hero get less experience for any such tactic?
>Usually you just get small amounts of experience points for "tactics" in
>Nethack. This huge gain is not for tactics but for the danger that comes
>with "special damages", as I interpret that comment.

Yes, and some tactics reduce the "special damage", and the risk involved.
You might as well additionally check for whether the hero has killed the
; with a missile, or whether he is wearing an amulet of magical
breathing, or polymorphed into a unbreathing creature. Special casing for
circumstances of a kill opens up a can of worms.

>You have overlooked that the 1000+ experience points *is* the special
>case in the source code. 

If you seek the special case for monster abilities to be removed in
general, this is an entirely different affair. I do think special
abilities leading to killing in another way than by loss of hit-points
should be taken into account for XP, possibly by a one-by-one evaluation
of a creature's deadliness.
